.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1056 AM PDT Sun Sep 13 2026
(Monday night through next Saturday)

Zonal flow to slight troughing continues through midweek before
another deeper trough pushes into the Oregon and Northern California
border. Temperatures remain relatively seasonal on Tuesday with
highs in the upper 70s to 80s across the interior and 60s along the
coastline. The arrival of the upper level trough will bring a cooler
airmass into the Bay Area and Central Coast with 850 mb temperatures
peaking around 12 C during the day. That is roughly between the 10th
and 25th percentiles for this time of year with the median 850 mb
temperature ~17 C. The NBM was running a bit warmer than other
models for Wednesday/Thursday but the latest run has started to
lower temperatures, coming into better agreement. A reasonable lower
end temperature forecast for Wed/Thurs would be interior highs in
the low to mid 70s while the higher end forecast (the current
forecast) keeps high temperatures in the mid 70s to low 80s. Given
the arrival of the much cooler airmass, would not be surprised if we
saw the forecast high temperatures for Wed/Thurs to decrease by a
few more degrees (slightly closer to the lower end forecast). The
caveat to this being temperatures may be warmer if the trough/cold
air mass slows down and arrives later than currently forecast.
Regardless, the cool down will be most evident Wednesday and
Thursday with near normal temperatures in the upper 70s to mid 80s
Friday into the weekend. The arrival of the trough should allow the
marine layer to deepen enough that stratus will become more
widespread mid to late week. Diurnally breezy winds continue across
mountain gaps and passes, the coast, and along the highest
ridgelines. Northern California and the Sierra Range have some
potential for thunderstorms mid to late week as the trough moves in
